\description{
Pools correlations across independent studies on the Fisher's \emph{Z}
scale and reports the results back in the correlation metric. Optionally,
each study's correlation is first corrected for attenuation due to
measurement error in either or both variables (the Spearman correction of
\code{\link{correction_for_attenuation}}, the basic artifact correction of
Hunter and Schmidt's psychometric meta-analysis), using reliabilities you
supply, for example from the \code{\link{reliability}} family. The
pooled quantity is then the correlation between the constructs rather
than the attenuated correlation between the observed scores, and the
weight of each study comes from the sampling variance of its corrected
correlation, which the correction enlarges.
}
\details{
Without reliabilities, pooling uses \eqn{z_i = \mathrm{atanh}(r_i)} with
sampling variance \eqn{1 / (n_i - 3)}; with them, the effect sizes and
their sampling variances are those given under Corrected correlations
below. Either way, the pooled estimate, its confidence limits, and the
prediction interval are transformed back through \eqn{\tanh}. The
heterogeneity quantities (tau, tau-squared, I-squared, H-squared, Q)
remain on the Fisher's \emph{Z} scale, where the model lives; tau is
therefore the between-study standard deviation of the Fisher's \emph{Z}
values.

\strong{Corrected correlations.} With \eqn{a_i = \rho_{xx,i}\,
\rho_{yy,i}}, the corrected correlation is
\eqn{r_{c,i} = r_i / \sqrt{a_i}} and the effect size pooled is
\eqn{\mathrm{atanh}(r_{c,i})}. The correction divides the sampling error
of \eqn{r_i} by \eqn{\sqrt{a_i}} along with \eqn{r_i} itself, so a
corrected correlation is less precise than the observed correlation it
came from, and the Fisher's \emph{Z} transformation, which makes the
sampling variance of \eqn{\mathrm{atanh}(r_i)} free of the population
correlation, does not do the same for \eqn{\mathrm{atanh}(r_{c,i})}. The
sampling variance used is the delta method approximation
\deqn{v_i = \frac{(1 - r_i^2)^2}{a_i (1 - r_{c,i}^2)^2 (n_i - 3)},}
which is \eqn{1 / (n_i - 3)}, the sampling variance of
\eqn{\mathrm{atanh}(r_i)}, times the square of the derivative of
\eqn{\mathrm{atanh}(r_{c,i})} with respect to \eqn{\mathrm{atanh}(r_i)}.
That derivative exceeds 1 whenever \eqn{a_i < 1}, and it grows without
bound as \eqn{r_{c,i}} approaches 1 in magnitude. It is evaluated at each
study's observed correlation, so \eqn{v_i} depends on that study alone,
as the model of \code{\link{meta_es}} takes the sampling variances to
be known. With no correction (\eqn{a_i = 1}), \eqn{v_i} is
\eqn{1 / (n_i - 3)} exactly. The \eqn{v_i} enter wherever the sampling
variances do: the weights, the pooled estimate with its standard error
and confidence interval, the between-study variance with its interval,
I-squared, H-squared, Q, and the prediction interval. For five studies
with \eqn{r = 0.40}, \eqn{n = 100}, and both reliabilities 0.80, each
corrected correlation is 0.50 and each \eqn{v_i} is 1.96 times
\eqn{1 / (n_i - 3)}; the fixed effect interval for the correlation
between the constructs is 0.401 to 0.588.

The reliabilities are treated as known constants. When they are
estimates, from the same samples or from other sources, their sampling
error is a further source of uncertainty in each corrected correlation
that \eqn{v_i} does not include, and the intervals are then narrower than
the total uncertainty warrants, by an amount that depends on how
precisely the reliabilities are known. Because \eqn{v_i} grows with the
magnitude of the study's own correlation, a study whose corrected
correlation is larger in magnitude receives less weight, which moves the
pooled estimate toward zero. In simulations run for this function (10
studies, a correlation of 0.5 between the constructs, reliabilities from
0.60 to 0.95, 30 to 400 observations per study, and 4,000 sets of
studies per condition, so that a coverage rate near 0.95 has a Monte
Carlo standard error of about 0.0035), the pooled estimate was, on
average, about 0.003 to 0.015 below the construct correlation on the
Fisher's \emph{Z} scale, depending on the condition. When the effects
were homogeneous, the fixed effect interval covered at the nominal 0.95
within Monte Carlo error (0.950 to 0.955; 0.80 to 0.83 with the variance
of an uncorrected correlation). With heterogeneous effects (a
between-study standard deviation of 0.15 on the Fisher's \emph{Z} scale
of the construct correlations), the interval for tau-squared covered at
0.95 to 0.97 (0.79 to 0.85 with the variance of an uncorrected
correlation) and the Hartung-Knapp interval at 0.93 to 0.95. The delta
method approximation is poorer when \eqn{n_i} is small or a corrected
correlation is near 1 in magnitude: for a correlation of 0.7 between the
constructs, reliabilities of 0.7, and 60 observations per study, a
corrected correlation reached 1 in 7 percent of the sets of studies,
and in the rest the pooled estimate was, on average, about 0.08 below
the construct correlation on the Fisher's \emph{Z} scale and the
intervals covered at 0.84 to 0.87. The artifact distribution methods of
Hunter and Schmidt (2004) are not implemented. A corrected correlation
that reaches or exceeds 1 in magnitude (possible when an observed
\eqn{r} outruns the supplied reliabilities) is an error at the pooling
stage, unlike the single-study \code{\link{correction_for_attenuation}},
which reports it with a warning: \eqn{\mathrm{atanh}} is undefined
there.
